      {
        "review_id": 580023969,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r580023969-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Wonderful temple",
        "text": "One of the ancient temples of Siva ,in Kathmandu , Pashupatinath defines Nepal in terms of hospitality , spirituality and devotion . It is believed that the temple was constructed in 400 BC ( source Wikepedia) ,but no definitive proof of that . It was renovated last in 15th Century by the then king . There are many legends surrounding the temple. One of it says that , Lord Siva and Parvati once his there in form of antelope but the Devtas found them . In the process a horn was broken and established there as Siva lingam . Once a cow starter showering milk on the lingam and people came to know about the sanctity of the place . The temple in the current form is built in pagoda style with the big Nandi in front and .sacro sanctum, or the main idol is a stone Mukhalinga with a silver yoni base bound with the silver serpent. The lingam is one metre high and has faced in four directions. These faces represent various aspects of Shiva; Sadyojata (also known as Barun), Vamdeva (also known as Ardha Nareshwor), Tatpurusha, Aghor & Ishana (imaginative). Facing West, North, East, South and Zenith respectively representing five primary elements namely earth, water, air, light and ether. Each face has tiny protruding hands holding rudraksha mala on right hand and a kamandalu on the other. Unlike other Shiva lingams in India and Nepal this Pashupati Shiva lingam is always Dressed in its golden vastra except during abhishakam, so pouring milk and Ganga Jal is only possible during abhishakam through the main priests. ( copied from Wikipedia)",
